    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. There's only one test class (`AcceptanceTest`) with 2 tests, both passing.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule: **creating an owner whose normalized telephone already belongs to another owner is rejected with HTTP 409**.\n\nTo keep structural impact low, I isolated the new behavior into a small cohesive unit rather than growing any existing method or class:\n\n1. **New file `DuplicateTelephoneException.java`** (in `rest.advice`) \u2014 a tiny class with almost no surrounding complexity (low `WMC_other`). It carries both its own telephone normalization (`replaceAll(\"\\\\D\",\"\")`) and the static `rejectIfDuplicate(...)` rule that scans existing owners and throws.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a single guard line (plus one import). The method's cyclomatic complexity stays at 1; the branching logic lives in the new class, not in this large controller.\n\n3. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added one small, self-contained `@ExceptionHandler` (CC 1) mapping the exception to `409 CONFLICT`, reusing the existing `detailBuild` helper for a consistent `ProblemDetail` body. This was necessary because the advice's catch-all `Exception` handler would otherwise turn it into a 500.\n\nOnly 3 files touched, each changed function kept simple (CC 1) with minimal changed lines, and the real logic placed where the surrounding complexity it must coexist with is near zero.",
